{
  // HL7 v2.5.1 masking configuration - one config for every v2.5.1 message type.
  //
  // HL7 segment layouts are defined per VERSION, not per message. PID-5 is the patient name in
  // an ADT^A01, an ORU^R01 and a DFT^P03 alike; message types only draw different subsets of the
  // same segments. So one config serves all 121 message types in the reference. That is the
  // opposite of X12, where an 837 and an 835 need separate configs because the same segment id
  // means different things in each.
  //
  // Every segment referenced by the 20 documented message structures is covered.
  //
  // SOURCES, which differ in how much they should be trusted:
  //
  //   The 31 segments in HL7_v2.5.1_Layout_Reference.xlsx take both their field layout and their
  //   PHI category from that workbook.
  //
  //   The other segments - IN2, IN3, ACC, PDA, IAM, UB1, UB2, DRG, RXA, RXO, ODS, ODT, RQD,
  //   TQ2, BLG, SPM, CTD, CON, SAC, CTI, DSC - take their FIELD LAYOUT from the HL7 v2.5.1
  //   standard text at hl7.eu/HL7v2x/v251/std251, but their PHI CATEGORY is a judgement applied
  //   here, following the same Safe Harbor categories the workbook uses. Those judgements are
  //   worth a review; the layouts are not guesses.
  //
  // REVIEWED AND DELIBERATELY NOT MASKED:
  //   TCD - all eight fields are dilution factors, repeat flags or a test code. Nothing to mask.
  //   RXR - route, site, device and method codes. Clinical, not identifying.
  //   RQ1 - vendor pricing and catalogue references, no patient link.
  //   SAC equipment, carrier and tray identifiers (5, 10, 13) name machine slots, not patients.
  //   CTI-2 and CTI-3 are study phase and time point codes; only the sponsor study id is masked.

  // =====================================================================================
  // HOW TO CHANGE THIS FILE
  //
  // Edits take effect when the engine next loads this file: at startup, or when somebody presses
  // "Reload configs" on the console's Run screen. Editing it while the engine runs changes nothing
  // until one of those happens, and a run already going keeps the config it started with - so a
  // masked file can be said to have been masked by one config from beginning to end.
  //
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------- POSITION IS OFF BY ONE
  //
  // "position" COUNTS THE SEGMENT ID AS 1. So position = the HL7 field number + 1. This is the
  // most common mistake made in this file and it is a silent one: a rule addressing a field that
  // happens to be empty matches nothing, and the run still reports success.
  //
  //     PID|1||MRN123456^^^GENHOSP^MR|
  //     ---  -  ---------
  //      |   |      +----- PID-3,  "position": 4
  //      |   +------------ PID-1,  "position": 2
  //      +---------------- the segment id, "position": 1
  //                        (PID-2 is the empty field between them, "position": 3)
  //
  // The comment above each block names the HL7 field; the position inside it is one higher. When a
  // rule seems to do nothing, check this first. The run prints a line for every rule that matched
  // nothing, and it names the address it was given rather than the field you meant:
  //
  //     no matches: PID-02.1  (PID position 3.1 -> alnumId)
  //
  // "component" is 1-based and has NO such offset: component 1 is the first component. Repetitions
  // need nothing said about them - a rule applies to every repetition separated by ~.
  //
  //     PID|1||MRN123456^^^GENHOSP^MR~MBR987654^^^PAYERX^MB
  //            ---------   -------    ---------   ------
  //                |          |
  //                |          +-- component 4, matched in BOTH repetitions
  //                +------------- component 1, matched in BOTH repetitions
  //
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------------ WORKED EXAMPLES
  //
  // CHANGE WHICH ALGORITHM MASKS A FIELD - edit the name and nothing else:
  //
  //     { "position": 6, "component": 1, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "lastName" } }
  //                                                                        --------
  //     Any algorithm or instance name the engine knows works here. If the name is not one of
  //     them the config is refused when it loads, with the full list printed, so a typo cannot
  //     reach a file.
  //
  // STOP MASKING A FIELD - delete its line. There is no "noMask" algorithm: a field with no rule
  // is retained exactly as it was, and deleting the line is how you say "leave this alone".
  //
  // MASK A FIELD NOTHING COVERS YET - add a line to that segment's block:
  //
  //     { "segment_id": "PID",
  //       // PID-3 Patient Identifier List [CX] - Identifier
  //       "maskFields": [
  //         { "position": 4, "component": 1, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "alnumId" } },
  //         { "position": 4, "component": 4, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "orgName" } } ] },
  //                       -             -                                     -------
  //                    PID-3   the assigning authority        a facility code, so orgName rather
  //                                                           than a person algorithm
  //
  //     Think about whether the value should move at all before adding a rule. The assigning
  //     authority above is the namespace that makes an MRN mean something: masking it keeps the
  //     join but changes what a downstream system reads as the issuing facility. It is
  //     deliberately NOT masked as this file stands, and a test pins that.
  //
  // MASK A SEGMENT WITH NO BLOCK AT ALL - add a whole one. Nothing in the Java knows these segment
  // names; every one of them comes from this file.
  //
  //     { "segment_id": "ZPD",
  //       "maskFields": [
  //         { "position": 2, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "memberId" } } ] },
  //
  // A RULE WITH NO "component" masks the whole field, separators and all. Use that for simple
  // fields - a date, an id - and name a component for composite ones like a name or an address.
  // =====================================================================================

  "record_delimiter":      "\r",
  "segment_delimiter":     "|",
  "component_delimiter":   "^",
  "repetition_delimiter":  "~",

  // No "subjects" block. This config is the profiling output - segment, PHI element, algorithm -
  // and it says nothing about whose dates are whose, so every date it shifts takes one offset for
  // the whole run. Every date in a message therefore moves together, MSH-7 and EVN-2 included; what
  // is gone is the per-patient, per-encounter and per-order separation, so one recovered date
  // recovers the rest.

  "maskSegments": [

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ MSH
    //
    // MSH-7 is the time the sending system built the message, which reads like envelope metadata -
    // the HL7 equivalent of an X12 ISA/GS date, and those are deliberately left alone.
    //
    // It is not the same case. An 837 is a batch assembled well after the care it bills, so its
    // interchange date is related to no one; a real-time ADT^A01 exists BECAUSE the admission
    // happened and goes out seconds later. In the reference sample MSH-7 is 15 seconds after EVN-2
    // and half an hour after the PV1-44 admit, so it IS the admission date kept somewhere else.
    // Left in the clear beside a shifted PV1-44 it hands over the encounter offset and unmasks
    // every other encounter date in the message.
    //
    // It follows the encounter, so a message keeps its internal chronology. Note that MSH is also
    // the subject-reset segment and the first segment there is: this rule only resolves because
    // subjects are resolved for the whole message before any rule runs.
    //
    // A deployment that genuinely ships batch files spanning many patients can drop this rule -
    // there MSH-7 is the batch run and relates to nobody.
    { "segment_id": "MSH",
      // MSH-7 Date/Time Of Message [TS] - Date
      "maskFields": [
        { "position": 7, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "ENCOUNTER_DATE" } } ] },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ EVN
    { "segment_id": "EVN",
      // EVN-2 Recorded Date/Time [TS] - Date
      "maskFields": [
        { "position": 3, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "ENCOUNTER_DATE" } } ] },
    { "segment_id": "EVN",
      // EVN-5 Operator ID [XCN] - Provider/Staff
      "maskFields": [
        { "position": 6, "component": 1, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "alnumId" } },
        { "position": 6, "component": 2, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "lastName" } },
        { "position": 6, "component": 3, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "firstName" } } ] },
    { "segment_id": "EVN",
      // EVN-6 Event Occurred [TS] - Date
      "maskFields": [
        { "position": 7, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "ENCOUNTER_DATE" } } ] },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ PID
    { "segment_id": "PID",
      // PID-2 Patient ID [CX] - Identifier
      "maskFields": [
        { "position": 3, "component": 1, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "alnumId" } } ] },
    { "segment_id": "PID",
      // PID-3 Patient Identifier List [CX] - Identifier
      "maskFields": [
        { "position": 4, "component": 1, "algorithm_reference": { "name": "alnumId" } } ] },
    { "segment_id": "PID",
